\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Every window flagged by the original region-max statistic, under both statistics, so the revision can be audited across all of them, not just the AU\nobreakspace  {}Mic window. $S_{\rm  reg}$: maximum of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar} over the $n_{\rm  src}=135$-position source region; `ctrl max': the largest of the 512{} single-position controls it was compared with; $T_\star $: the symmetric single-position statistic at the propagated stellar position; `ring max': that window's largest control statistic; $\nu $: the window centre. For CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 the region maximum already lay on the star, so the two statistics coincide exactly.}}{21}{table.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bothstats}{{11}{21}{Every window flagged by the original region-max statistic, under both statistics, so the revision can be audited across all of them, not just the AU~Mic window. $S_{\rm reg}$: maximum of Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar} over the $n_{\rm src}=135$-position source region; `ctrl max': the largest of the \NCtrl {} single-position controls it was compared with; $T_\star $: the symmetric single-position statistic at the propagated stellar position; `ring max': that window's largest control statistic; $\nu $: the window centre. For CP$-$72~2713 the region maximum already lay on the star, so the two statistics coincide exactly}{table.11}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The AU\nobreakspace  {}Mic crossing frequency (230.8252\,GHz) under the three treatments that decide it: the release's frozen products (region-max statistic), the fully symmetric reprocessing of the same first-epoch block (2014 August\nobreakspace  {}18, observatory calibration recipe verbatim) and the second-epoch block of the same programme (2014 June\nobreakspace  {}5, identical search treatment). The peak drift is recomputed over a differently weighted cube in each column, so the $\sim $10--20 per cent spread measures the drift-location uncertainty of a $\lesssim 2.5\sigma $ per-channel feature (Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:inject} measures exactly this effect), not physical drift evolution -- and a drift-location error of this size cannot change a disposition, which rests on amplitude and rank.}}{21}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:aumic}{{12}{21}{The AU~Mic crossing frequency (230.8252\,GHz) under the three treatments that decide it: the release's frozen products (region-max statistic), the fully symmetric reprocessing of the same first-epoch block (2014 August~18, observatory calibration recipe verbatim) and the second-epoch block of the same programme (2014 June~5, identical search treatment). The peak drift is recomputed over a differently weighted cube in each column, so the $\sim $10--20 per cent spread measures the drift-location uncertainty of a $\lesssim 2.5\sigma $ per-channel feature (Appendix~\ref {app:inject} measures exactly this effect), not physical drift evolution -- and a drift-location error of this size cannot change a disposition, which rests on amplitude and rank}{table.12}{}}
