\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Nominal EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ trigger thresholds (boxed rule, \S  \ref  {sec:method}) against distance, one deepest window per star and band (107{} datasets, 88{} stars). Symbols mark whether that window is Class A (drift-resolved), where the injection campaign measures end-to-end recovery of the drifting class, or Class B (unresolved excess), where detection completeness is measured post-baseline but the drift axis is undiscriminating (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:classcomp}); survey-wide the split is 118{} Class A against 313{} Class B windows. Stars mark the four windows with a stage-1 statistical flag (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:flagged}). The dashed line is an Arecibo-like planetary radar, $2\times 10^{13}$\,W, a scale for human transmitter power, not a claim that the survey reaches Earth-technology detectability (\S  \ref  {sec:benchmarks}).}}{22}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:eirp}{{9}{22}{Nominal EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ trigger thresholds (boxed rule, \S \ref {sec:method}) against distance, one deepest window per star and band (\NStarBands {} datasets, \NStars {} stars). Symbols mark whether that window is Class A (drift-resolved), where the injection campaign measures end-to-end recovery of the drifting class, or Class B (unresolved excess), where detection completeness is measured post-baseline but the drift axis is undiscriminating (Table~\ref {tab:classcomp}); survey-wide the split is \NFine {} Class A against \NCoarse {} Class B windows. Stars mark the four windows with a stage-1 statistical flag (Table~\ref {tab:flagged}). The dashed line is an Arecibo-like planetary radar, $2\times 10^{13}$\,W, a scale for human transmitter power, not a claim that the survey reaches Earth-technology detectability (\S \ref {sec:benchmarks})}{figure.9}{}}
